WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.099
Meetings and Presentations.

2
00:00:02.379 --> 00:00:06.369
Day 68 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Workplace phase.

3
00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:09.143
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.423 --> 00:00:12.388
If you don't speak in meetings, people won't know what you know.

5
00:00:12.668 --> 00:00:18.407
Speaking up is part of the job, and so is running a meeting well and giving a demo people remember.

6
00:00:19.757 --> 00:00:20.610
Part 1.

7
00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.045
How to speak up (when you find it hard).

8
00:00:24.295 --> 00:00:25.489
Prepare one point:

9
00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:29.631
Read the agenda beforehand and write one question or opinion.

10
00:00:29.911 --> 00:00:30.871
Speak early:

11
00:00:31.151 --> 00:00:33.113
The first 5 minutes are the easiest.

12
00:00:33.393 --> 00:00:34.801
Waiting makes it harder.

13
00:00:35.081 --> 00:00:36.148
Build on others:

14
00:00:36.428 --> 00:00:39.287
Adding to what Meena said... is a low-risk way in.

15
00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:40.719
Ask a question:

16
00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:43.580
A good question counts as a contribution:

17
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.311
How will we measure success?

18
00:00:46.661 --> 00:00:48.559
Phrases to enter a conversation.

19
00:00:48.839 --> 00:00:50.183
Can I add something here?

20
00:00:50.463 --> 00:00:51.807
Building on that point...

21
00:00:52.087 --> 00:00:53.815
I have a slightly different view.

22
00:00:54.095 --> 00:00:56.527
Before we move on, can I raise one risk?

23
00:00:56.807 --> 00:00:58.109
Sorry to interrupt.

24
00:00:58.389 --> 00:00:59.669
Quick clarification:

25
00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:02.448
When someone interrupts you.

26
00:01:02.728 --> 00:01:03.667
You could say:

27
00:01:03.947 --> 00:01:08.171
Sorry, Rahul, let me just finish this point, then I'd love to hear yours.

28
00:01:09.521 --> 00:01:10.395
Part 2.

29
00:01:10.675 --> 00:01:11.721
Running a meeting.

30
00:01:12.971 --> 00:01:14.379
Purpose in the invite:

31
00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:17.411
Decide on X or Review Y (not Discuss).

32
00:01:17.691 --> 00:01:19.312
Agenda with time boxes.

33
00:01:19.592 --> 00:01:21.768
Only the people needed to make the decision.

34
00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:24.821
Start on time; recap the goal in the first minute.

35
00:01:25.101 --> 00:01:26.573
Park off-topic items:

36
00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:27.813
Great point.

37
00:01:28.093 --> 00:01:29.459
Let's take that offline.

38
00:01:29.739 --> 00:01:33.387
End 5 minutes early with decisions + owners + dates.

39
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.117
Send notes within an hour.

40
00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:37.363
Part 3.

41
00:01:37.643 --> 00:01:39.265
Demos and presentations.

42
00:01:40.515 --> 00:01:41.411
Structure.

43
00:01:41.691 --> 00:01:44.635
Hook (30 s): the problem, in the user's words.

44
00:01:44.915 --> 00:01:47.304
Customers kept asking: where's my order?

45
00:01:47.584 --> 00:01:51.424
Show (most of the time): the live demo, following the happy path.

46
00:01:51.704 --> 00:01:54.307
Impact: numbers, or what's now possible.

47
00:01:54.587 --> 00:01:56.016
Next steps or asks.

48
00:01:56.296 --> 00:01:57.213
Q and A.

49
00:01:58.563 --> 00:01:59.267
Tips.

50
00:01:59.547 --> 00:02:01.019
Rehearse twice out loud.

51
00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:03.731
Once alone, once with a friend or AI.

52
00:02:04.011 --> 00:02:07.787
Have a backup: screenshots or a recording in case the demo breaks.

53
00:02:08.067 --> 00:02:13.849
Increase font size (Ctrl or Cmd +) and close Slack and email notifications.

54
00:02:14.129 --> 00:02:15.558
One message per slide.

55
00:02:15.838 --> 00:02:17.758
Slide titles should be sentences:

56
00:02:18.038 --> 00:02:20.683
Search is 3 times faster, not Results.

57
00:02:20.963 --> 00:02:22.435
Pause after key points.

58
00:02:22.715 --> 00:02:24.166
Silence makes them stick.

59
00:02:25.516 --> 00:02:26.817
Handling Q and A.

60
00:02:27.097 --> 00:02:28.463
Here is a quick table:

61
00:02:28.743 --> 00:02:30.471
Situation, and Response.

62
00:02:30.751 --> 00:02:32.287
Good question you can answer:

63
00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:35.873
Answer in 1 to 3 sentences, then Does that answer it?

64
00:02:36.153 --> 00:02:37.049
You don't know:

65
00:02:37.329 --> 00:02:40.935
I don't know, but I'll find out and send it to everyone by tomorrow.

66
00:02:41.215 --> 00:02:42.431
Hostile question:

67
00:02:42.711 --> 00:02:43.991
That's a fair concern.

68
00:02:44.271 --> 00:02:46.084
Then answer calmly, with facts.

69
00:02:46.364 --> 00:02:47.324
Off-topic:

70
00:02:47.604 --> 00:02:48.692
Great question.

71
00:02:48.972 --> 00:02:50.935
Can we take that offline after this?

72
00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:52.687
Long rambling question:

73
00:02:52.967 --> 00:02:55.335
If I understand, you're asking about X.

74
00:02:55.615 --> 00:02:56.617
Is that right?

75
00:02:57.967 --> 00:02:58.885
Part 4.

76
00:02:59.165 --> 00:03:00.551
Video call etiquette.

77
00:03:01.801 --> 00:03:04.255
Camera on for one-on-ones and small meetings.

78
00:03:04.535 --> 00:03:07.479
Mute when not speaking; unmute before you start talking.

79
00:03:07.759 --> 00:03:10.959
Look at the camera, not the screen, when making your main point.

80
00:03:11.239 --> 00:03:14.225
Use the chat for links, not side conversations.

81
00:03:14.505 --> 00:03:17.044
Say your name before speaking in large calls:

82
00:03:17.324 --> 00:03:18.305
Ashish here.

83
00:03:18.585 --> 00:03:19.631
Quick question.

84
00:03:20.981 --> 00:03:21.941
Part 5.

85
00:03:22.221 --> 00:03:23.607
Practice conversation:

86
00:03:23.887 --> 00:03:25.850
Handling a tough question in a demo.

87
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.127
Here is how the conversation goes.

88
00:03:29.407 --> 00:03:33.332
This looks good, but what happens if the payment gateway goes down during a sale?

89
00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:35.212
That's a fair concern.

90
00:03:35.492 --> 00:03:40.420
We retry three times, and then we switch to a backup gateway automatically.

91
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.449
How long does the switch take?

92
00:03:42.729 --> 00:03:44.265
Under five seconds.

93
00:03:44.545 --> 00:03:46.487
Customers don't see an error.

94
00:03:47.837 --> 00:03:49.437
The conversation continues.

95
00:03:49.717 --> 00:03:51.039
Have you tested it?

96
00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:54.199
Yes, we tested it last week with fake failures.

97
00:03:54.479 --> 00:03:56.869
I can share the results after the meeting.

98
00:03:57.149 --> 00:03:58.130
Please do.

99
00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.016
That's the end of this lesson.

100
00:04:01.296 --> 00:04:05.968
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

101
00:04:06.248 --> 00:04:12.349
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

